        "type": "tekst",
        "titel": "Step 0. What you are actually making",
        "toetsstof": true,
        "tekst": "Before the steps, one picture to keep in your head.\n\nA summary is **a short, independent text that tells a reader who has not read the article what the article's [[main points|Main point]] are**, in your own words, in an academic register, organised by you.\n\nThree things follow from that sentence:\n\n**\"A reader who has not read the article.\"** Your reader has no background. If the article talks about \"ASI\", your reader needs to know what it is. If the article is about a debate, your reader needs to know there **is** a debate, and between whom.\n\n**\"Main points.\"** Not every point. An article of 1,000 to 1,500 words contains far more than fits in 500 of yours. Selecting is half the job, and the content criterion punishes both missing main points and including unimportant ones.\n\n**\"Organised by you.\"** Not in the order of the article. A news article is written to keep you scrolling: the most dramatic claim first, the qualifications later, quotes scattered everywhere. You reorganise it by **theme**.\n\n**What a summary is not** (session 2): not a paraphrase of the whole article sentence by sentence; not a commentary with your opinion; not a review that talks about the article.\n\n**The shape you are aiming for**, with 450 to 550 words:\n\n**Main points list** (not counted): one line per paragraph.\n**Paragraph 1, the opening** (about 90 to 110 words): what the subject is, the [[topic sentence for the whole summary|Topic sentence for the whole summary]], minimal context.\n**Paragraphs 2 and 3, the body** (about 120 to 140 words each): one theme each, five moves each.\n**Paragraph 4, the final paragraph** (about 100 to 120 words): the opposing sides.\n\nFour paragraphs is the natural shape for 450 to 550 words. Three works if the article has only two [[strands|Strand]]; five if it has four, but then each paragraph gets thin."
