        "tekst": "**Goal:** after this step you can say in **one sentence** what the whole article is about. Nothing more.\n\n**How to read, in three passes.**\n\n**Pass 1, fast (about 5 minutes for a long article).** Read the headline, the subheading, the first two paragraphs and the last two paragraphs. Then skim the rest, looking only at the first sentence of each paragraph. Do not underline anything yet.\n\n**Pass 2, carefully.** Read everything. Now mark, in the margin, what **kind** of thing each paragraph is: a claim (C), a reason or evidence (R), a reaction or counterargument (X), background (B), an example or story (E). This is quicker than underlining and tells you how the article is built.\n\n**Pass 3, only if needed.** Re-read the parts you did not understand.\n\n**Then answer four questions**, in your head or in three words each on scrap paper:\n\n1. **What is the subject?** (the thing: a technology, a policy, an event)\n2. **What is being said about it?** (the central claim or development)\n3. **Who disagrees, or what is the other side?** (almost every exam article is a debate: \"experts warn, but critics say\")\n4. **Where does it end?** (the current situation, the open question)\n\n**Write the [[gist|Gist]] sentence.** One sentence, in your own words, that answers 1, 2 and 3:\n\n*Example (from your own assignment):* \"Some experts warn that superintelligent AI could threaten humanity and call for regulation, while others think the danger is exaggerated.\"\n\nThat sentence is not part of your summary yet, but it will become the core of your opening paragraph. If you cannot write it, read pass 2 again: you do not understand the article yet, and no amount of good writing will fix that."
