        "tekst": "**Goal:** the list that goes at the top of your summary, written **before** the summary itself. One line per paragraph, in the order of your paragraphs.\n\n**Why first?** Because it is your plan. If you cannot write a line for a paragraph, you do not know yet what that paragraph is for.\n\n**How to write a line.** Each line is the **[[main point|Main point]]** of that paragraph in about 10 to 20 words: not a title (\"Threats\"), not a whole paragraph. The best lines are close to the paragraph's [[topic sentence|Topic sentence]], in shorter form.\n\n**Format.** The assignment says \"list the main point per paragraph at the top of the summary\". A simple format:\n\n*Main points*\n*Paragraph 1: ...*\n*Paragraph 2: ...*\n*Paragraph 3: ...*\n*Paragraph 4: ...*\n\nThis list is not a heading inside the summary; it comes before it. The summary itself starts after the list, without a title. (Your first assignment used \"Main points\" and \"Summary\" as labels and scored 16/20; if you want to be safe, keep the label above the list and leave the summary itself without a heading.)\n\n**Templates for the lines:**\n\n- *Paragraph 1: [subject] and the central debate about [issue].*\n- *Paragraph 2: the [risks / causes / effects] of [subject], [the key specific].*\n- *Paragraph 3: the [responses / proposals / measures] to [problem], [the key specific].*\n- *Paragraph 4: the disagreement between [side A] and [side B] about [what separates them].*\n\n**Check:** your lines must match your paragraphs exactly. The lecturer's test (session 2): read only the topic sentences of your summary in a row; they should say the same as the list."
