        "toetsstof": true,
        "tekst": "This is where most people get stuck, so here it is completely spelled out. The opening paragraph has **four sentences** (three to five is fine) and about 90 to 110 words. It contains **no figures, no named positions and no details**: those belong in the body.\n\n**Sentence 1: what the subject is.** Name the subject and, if the reader cannot be expected to know it, define or describe it. If the subject is well known (social media, climate change), say instead what is happening to it now.\n\nTemplates:\n- *[Subject] is [a definition in your own words].*\n- *[Subject], [a short description], has become [the development].*\n- *[Subject] has increasingly [the development that makes it newsworthy].*\n\n**Sentence 2: why it is being discussed.** The concern, the change, the event that makes this a topic now. Often it starts with the subject again or \"This\", which links it to sentence 1.\n\nTemplates:\n- *Its rapid development has raised concerns about [issue].*\n- *This development has prompted [a reaction] from [group in general terms].*\n\n**Sentence 3: the [[topic sentence for the whole summary|Topic sentence for the whole summary]].** This is the most important sentence in the text. It states the **subject** plus the **[[strands|Strand]]** you will treat, **in the order of your paragraphs** (session 2). It is the statement of purpose that the rubric explanation asks for.\n\nTemplates:\n- *The debate centres on [strand 1, paragraph 2], [strand 2, paragraph 3] and [strand 3, paragraph 4].*\n- *[Group A] warn that [strand 1] and call for [strand 2], whereas [group B] argue that [strand 3].*\n- *These concerns involve [strand 1], [strand 2] and [the disagreement about strand 3].*\n\n**Sentence 4 (optional): the minimum context.** What kind of claim it is, who is involved in general terms, what is at stake. Still no figures and no names.\n\nTemplates:\n- *The issue involves [types of actors], including [general group] and [general group].*\n- *What is at stake is [the consequence in general terms].*\n\n**Check before you go on:**\n1. Does sentence 3 name every body paragraph, in the right order? Then join 1 of session 2 holds.\n2. Is there any number, name or detail in this paragraph? Move it to the body.\n3. Could your reader, after this paragraph alone, say what the debate is about? Then it works."
